First, ensure MongoDB Database Tools (mongodump, mongorestore) are installed and the required Atlas network access and permissions are configured.

Managed Service Case (Example MongoDB Atlas)

For Backup:
mongodump \
  --uri="" \
  --archive=archivename.gz \
  --gzip

For Restore:
mongorestore \
  --uri="" \
  --archive=archivename.gz \
  --gzip

For Restore & Replace Existing Data:
mongorestore \ 
  --uri="<mongodb-atlas-uri>" \ 
  --archive=backup.gz \ 
  --gzip \ 
  --drop

--drop removes existing collections before restoring them.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
Container running on Server Case

Step 1: Create a Backup

Run the backup command inside the MongoDB container:

docker exec container-name mongodump \
  --username <username> \
  --password '<password>' \
  --authenticationDatabase admin \
  --archive=/tmp/mongodb-backup.gz \
  --gzip

docker exec container-name ls -lh /tmp/mongodb-backup.gz

Step 2: Copy the Backup from the Container

Copy the backup from the Docker container to the server:

docker cp \
container-name:/tmp/mongodb-backup.gz \
/home/ubuntu/mongodb-backup.gz

# Step 3: Remove the Backup from the Container (Optional)

docker exec container-name rm -f /tmp/mongodb-backup.gz

# Step 4: Transfer the Backup to Your Local Machine

Run the following command from your local Linux machine:

scp -i ~/.ssh/file.pem \
ubuntu@<SERVER_IP>:/home/ubuntu/mongodb-backup.gz \
.

# Step 5: Create a Temporary MongoDB Container for Testing

docker run -d \
  --name mongodb-backup-test \
  -p 27018:27017 \
  mongo:8

# Step 6: Copy the Backup into the Test Container

docker cp \
mongodb-backup.gz \
mongodb-backup-test:/tmp/mongodb-backup.gz

# Step 7: Restore the Backup

docker exec mongodb-backup-test mongorestore \
  --archive=/tmp/mongodb-backup.gz \
  --gzip

# Step 8: Verify Databases

List restored databases:

docker exec mongodb-backup-test \
mongosh --quiet --eval "show dbs"


